C = [
("h1", "4. The Emptying and the Name"),
("p", "Claim: Philippians 2:7 and 2:8 say what he did, and 2:9 to 2:11 say what God did. The verses do not list what was set aside, and the name given at the end is a name that Isaiah 45 reserves for the LORD. The volume reads both with care and stops where the passage stops."),
("sub", "4.1 “Made himself of no reputation”"),
("p", "The King James Version has “But made himself of no reputation, and took upon him the form of a servant, and was made in the likeness of men” (Philippians 2:7). The verb is active and its subject is Christ: he made himself. Two things follow, and the sentence adds nothing to them. The first is that he took “the form of a servant,” which is something added. The second is that he was “made in the likeness of men.” The grammar of the verse tells how he did it: by taking, and by being made."),
("p", "Many readers have asked what he emptied himself of. Constable reports that the verb names an act and does not name a thing: “Paul did not say that Jesus emptied Himself of some thing. He simply said that He emptied Himself.” He adds the matching point: “He added on the form of a human being.” The volume takes the same line. The passage names an act of self-giving and the taking of a form. It does not name an attribute that was put away."),
("sub", "4.2 The theory of an emptying"),
("p", "A long tradition has built a theory on the verse, that in becoming man he gave up some of what belongs to God, such as knowledge, glory or the use of power. The volume does not adopt it and does not rule it out by a counter-theory. It notes that the verse offers the theory no list, and that the two clauses on either side give the manner: taking and being made. N. T. Wright goes further. He writes that the question of what Jesus emptied himself of could be asked in the usual way only if one were committed to “a quite unbiblical view of God.” The volume cites him as a writer who objects to the usual way of putting the question, and does not follow his reason, which rests on a reading of the whole story of Israel that the volume does not argue."),
("sub", "4.3 The humbling and the death"),
("p", "The verse that follows is plain: “And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross” (Philippians 2:8). The steps are four: found in fashion as a man, humbled himself, obedient unto death, the death of the cross. Each is a step down, and the last is the lowest. The Cross is named here as the lowest point of an example, not as an act of atonement. The volume on the Cross reads what the Cross did in other passages. Here it is the end of the descent."),
("sub", "4.4 The name above every name"),
("p", "“Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name” (Philippians 2:9). The subject has changed to God. The name is not stated in the verse. The next verse says, “That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow,” and the one after, “that Jesus Christ is Lord” (Philippians 2:10, 11). So the name is either Jesus or Lord; the passage uses both, and it does not say which is meant."),
("p", "The bowing of every knee and the confession of every tongue are the language of Isaiah 45. There the LORD says, “Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth: for I am God, and there is none else,” and then, “That unto me every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear” (Isaiah 45:22, 23). Paul writes the same two acts, a bowing and a confession, and says they will be made at the name of Jesus. The volume does not say that Paul quotes Isaiah or that he intends the whole of Isaiah 45. It says that the two passages share two acts, that in Isaiah the acts are rendered to the LORD, and that in Philippians they are rendered at the name of Jesus “to the glory of God the Father.” Wright, who calls the hymn a use of “fiercely monotheistic theology of Isaiah 40-55,” reads the same pairing, and the volume agrees with him as to the pairing."),
("sub", "4.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The verb of 2:7 names an act, not a thing set aside, and the passage lists no attribute.",
    "The theory of an emptying is not in the text, and the volume does not build one.",
    "The Cross appears here as the lowest step of the example.",
    "The exaltation is God’s act, and the name is either Jesus or Lord, and the passage does not say which.",
    "The bowing and the confession are the acts Isaiah 45 gives to the LORD, and Philippians gives them at the name of Jesus, to the glory of God the Father.",
  ]),
("p", "The chapter ends where the passage ends. It does not say how the one who was in the form of God is the one who bows to no one but is bowed to. It says that Paul wrote both sentences, in one hymn."),
]
